StructuredText.Text:
// Called by the CPU when an I/O module raises a diagnostic: its 24 V supply
// failing, a DQ output short-circuited to L+ or to ground, a parameter error, or
// a wire break where that is ever enabled. Those diagnostics were switched on
// across all 36 modules on 2026-09-06 - they ship DISABLED, which is why nothing
// would have reached this block even if it had existed earlier.
//
// WHY THIS BLOCK DOES NOT DECODE #IO_State
//
// The same OB fires when a fault APPEARS and again when it GOES AWAY, and
// #IO_State is what separates the two. Its bit meanings could not be confirmed
// from any reachable Siemens source, and this block cannot be tested here:
// PLCSIM does not simulate module-level diagnostics, for the same reason it does
// not simulate the station events OB86 handles (handoff item 11).
//
// Guessing the polarity would risk precisely the failure OB86's own comment
// warns about - clearing the flag on failure and setting it on recovery, giving
// a system that reports healthy exactly when it is not. So this block does not
// guess. ModuleFault LATCHES on any event and clears only via ModuleFaultAck.
//
// The worst case is a stale fault sitting after a module has recovered: visible,
// irritating, and safe. The opposite error is silent. UnexpMove and DirFault
// already latch for the same reason.
//
// Everything is stored RAW and undecoded so the bit meanings can be LEARNED from
// the real panel rather than guessed now: pull a module and read
// LastModuleIoState, plug it back in and read it again. Two observations settle
// what the documentation would not.

"Diag_DB".ModuleFault := TRUE;

// Hand the event to FB_ValveLoop rather than working out which module it was here.
// The decode needs a 36-entry lookup, and this block cannot be triggered in PLCSIM -
// so doing it here would ship untestable logic. In FB_ValveLoop the same decode can be
// exercised from a watch table by writing a LADDR and setting this flag by hand.
"Diag_DB".NewModuleEvent := TRUE;

#id := #LADDR;
"Diag_DB".LastModuleLaddr := #id;
"Diag_DB".LastModuleIoState := #IO_State;
"Diag_DB".LastModuleChannel := #Channel;
"Diag_DB".LastModuleMultiError := #MultiError;

// Saturating, not wrapping. An Int rolling past 32767 into negative would read as
// a different kind of fault to whoever is looking at it, and the exact count that
// far out says nothing the latched flag has not already said. What the count is
// genuinely for is spotting a module that is CHATTERING - rising steadily while
// ModuleFault has never been cleared.
IF "Diag_DB".ModuleEventCount < 32767 THEN
    "Diag_DB".ModuleEventCount := "Diag_DB".ModuleEventCount + 1;
END_IF;
